      "thinking": "Let me analyze this markdown. It's interesting — the actual content is not raw OCR of the certificate, but a summary of page 2 provided by some LLM (\"I've noted the contents of page 2...\"), and page 3 was blocked.\n\nLet me extract what I can:\n\n- Certificate number: R76/2006-GB1-12.10 — scheme would be... R76 is the recommendation. The certificate number format \"R76/2006-GB1-12.10\" suggests OIML R76-1/2006 edition. Issued presumably by GB1 (UK? Actually GB1 might be NWML/Trading Standards...). Actually the \"GB1\" is the issuing authority code for United Kingdom.\n\n- Page 2 of 3, so page_total = 3.\n\n- Evaluation Report P00917 (13 pages) — test report.\n\n- Instruments: non-automatic weighing instruments, family of models using A-Series and V-Series digital indicators: AFS, AFM, ABM, ATM, APS, VFS, VFM, VBM, VTM, VPS models.\n\n- Class III or IIII, mains or battery-powered, self-indicating, single or dual interval/range, direct sales to the public.\n\n- Features: stainless steel enclosures (AFS, APS, VFS, VPS), plastic enclosures (AFM, ATM, ABM, VFM, VBM, VTM), 2.0\" or 1.0\" graphic LCD displays depending on model, A/D conversion module, main board with processor, 4-wire (A-Series) or 6-wire (V-Series) load cell connections.\n\n- Functions: zero setting/tracking, subtractive tare, stability of equilibrium, zero and net indicators, memory/totalisation, printing.\n\n- Load cell requirements: R60 OIML certificate, compatibility of modules, NH-marked cells only with R76 humidity testing, etc.\n\nNow let me build the JSON:\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R76/2006-GB1-12.10\"\n- scheme: \"A\" — hmm, scheme A vs B. Not explicitly stated. R76 certificates for complete instruments... The certificate number format \"R76/2006-GB1-12.10\" — typically scheme A certificates. But not stated in the text. Rule 8: don't fabricate. Omit or set null. I'll omit... Actually the schema lists scheme (A|B). If not present, omit. I'll omit.\n- page_total: 3\n- date_issued: not stated. Omit.\n- member_state: \"GB1\" hints UK but not stated explicitly. Hmm, \"GB1\" is in the certificate number. Could infer member state GB (United Kingdom). I think it's reasonable to leave member_state out or set null. The code GB1 is the issuer ID for the UK authority. Not explicitly stated though. I'll leave it out to avoid fabrication. Actually, hmm. The oiml_issuer_id could be \"GB1\" as it appears in certificate number. That's a reasonable inference — the certificate number contains the issuing authority code. I'll set oiml_issuer_id: \"GB1\". Actually, let me be careful. \"GB1\" is a country/authority code. I'll include it as oiml_issuer_id.\n\nissuing_authority: not named in the text. Omit fields or provide minimal. I'll omit the whole issuing_authority? The schema expects it but rule 8 says don't fabricate. I can include with name omitted... Better to just omit fields not present. Maybe include oiml_issuer_id: \"GB1\" there too? That's derived from cert number. I'll include issuing_authority with oiml_issuer_id \"GB1\"? Hmm, might be cleaner to omit the issuing_authority entirely. But the schema has it as a layer. I'll include it with just oiml_issuer_id.\n\napplicants: not stated. Omit.\n\nmanufacturers: not stated. Omit.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\" (per R76). Category examples list includes \"Measuring system\" etc. I'll use \"Non-automatic weighing instrument\".\n- type_designations: the model names: [\"AFS\", \"AFM\", \"ABM\", \"ATM\", \"APS\", \"VFS\", \"VFM\", \"VBM\", \"VTM\", \"VPS\"]\n- module_designation: these are indicators... The certified type is the indicator family used in weighing instruments. Actually the cert seems to cover indicators (A-Series and V-Series digital indicators) as modules for non-automatic weighing instruments. Hmm. The description says \"A family of non-automatic weighing instruments using A-Series and V-Series digital indicators connected to a weighing platform\". So the certificate covers indicators as modules? R76/2006-GB1-12.10 — GB1 issued by NWML. Many R76 certs for indicators exist. Given it mentions \"connected to a weighing platform\" and load cell compatibility requirements, this is likely a certificate for digital indicators (module) under R76. So category could be \"Indicator\". Hmm.\n\nActually, R76/2006-GB1-12.10 — this looks like a certificate for indicators (R76-1 is about indicators? No, R76-1 is metrological requirements for NAWSIs; R76-2 is for... Actually R76-1 covers indicators too as modules). The compatibility of the modules declaration mention suggests it's a module certificate (indicator). The category per schema: \"Indicator\" is listed. I think \"Indicator\" is appropriate, with description noting they're used in non-automatic weighing instruments connected to a weighing platform.\n\nHmm, but the summary says \"Instrument characteristics: A family of non-automatic weighing instruments using A-Series and V-Series digital indicators ... connected to a weighing platform\". This phrasing suggests the certified type is indicators intended for use in NAWSIs. I'll set category: \"Indicator\" and description accordingly.\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: null (maybe \"A-Series and V-Series indicators\")\n- models: one entry per variant: AFS, AFM, ABM, ATM, APS, VFS, VFM, VBM, VTM, VPS.\n\nattributes per model:\n- enclosure: stainless steel for AFS, APS, VFS, VPS; plastic for AFM, ATM, ABM, VFM, VBM, VTM\n- display: 2.0\" or 1.0\" graphic LCD depending on model — but we don't know which model has which. Can't assign per model. Hmm. It says \"2.0\" or 1.0\" graphic LCD displays depending on model\". We don't know the mapping. So this becomes a type_level characteristic with value as a list? Rule says lists of values of numeric Emax are model matrix, but here we can't map. I could put it as type_level with value string \"2.0\\\" or 1.0\\\" graphic LCD\". Or omit. I'll put a type-level characteristic: display: